//! lotus/replace.rye — ALES115: replace — remove the `count` samples starting at `at` and insert `src` in their place, IN PLACE, so [at, at+src.len) becomes `src`, everything
//! before `at` is untouched, everything after the removed span follows the new audio unchanged, and the length becomes len − count + src.len. This is the GENERAL span-edit — the
//! one gesture that ties ALES2's cut/splice and ALES114's paste_over together: it is exactly cut(at, count) then splice(at, src), composed over ALES2's OWN public API, inventing
//! no new transport, buffer, or error. Paste-into-a-selection is the button every editor ships — circle a span, drop different audio in its place, and the take changes length by
//! however much longer or shorter the new audio is. Fix a flubbed bar with a shorter clean take and the song tightens; drop a fuller phrase over a thin one and it lengthens.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: replace(clip, at, count, src) removes [at, at+count) and inserts `src` at `at`. It checks ALL bounds BEFORE any write — BadRange when the removed span
//! falls outside the current samples (at > len, or count > len − at, without underflow), and ClipFull when the result would run past the fixed buffer (src.len > buf.len − len +
//! count) — then composes ALES2's cut then splice, each pre-validated to succeed, so a refusal never lands mid-edit. It reuses ALES2's EditError whole.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE REPLACE LAW: the span [at, at+src.len) holds `src` exactly, the head is untouched, the tail after the removed span follows the new audio unchanged, and
//! the length is len − count + src.len (checked shorter, longer, and equal). THE GENERALIZATION LAW: an empty `src` equals ALES2's cut byte for byte; a zero `count` equals ALES2's
//! splice byte for byte; a `src.len == count` equals ALES114's paste_over byte for byte — one law, three faces. THE LENGTH LAW: the length changes by exactly src.len − count — it
//! shrinks when the replacement is shorter, grows when longer, holds when equal. THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW: replace(clip, at, 0, empty) is the identity; a removed span past the
//! samples BadRange, a result past the buffer ClipFull, each before any write.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES115.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-014021_fill-ales115-lotus-replace.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, the cut and splice this rung composes, and the EditError reused whole; lotus/paste_over.rye (ALES114) —
//! the length-preserving overwrite this rung generalizes (src.len == count), imported by the selftest to prove one corner of the generalization law; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the
//! peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to read the result.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. A bounded in-process buffer of i16 PCM on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It removes an existing span and writes caller-supplied samples
//! in its place, changing the length by a bounded amount and reading no byte past len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/replace.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");
const paste_over = @import("paste_over.rye");

pub const replace_version = "20260815.014021";

/// replace forwards ALES2's OWN faults reused by name — BadRange when the removed span falls outside the current samples, ClipFull when the result would overflow the fixed buffer —
/// and invents none of its own; `at`, `count`, and the payload length are plain position/span/size bounds with no illegal value save the ranges the samples and the buffer name.
pub const ReplaceError = timeline.EditError;

/// Remove the `count` samples starting at `at` and insert `src` in their place, in place — [at, at+src.len) becomes `src`, the head is untouched, the tail after the removed span
/// follows the new audio unchanged, and the length becomes len − count + src.len. All bounds are checked before any write; then it composes ALES2's cut then splice. This is the
/// general span-edit that generalizes cut (empty src), splice (zero count), and ALES114's paste_over (src.len == count).
pub fn replace(clip: *timeline.Clip, at: u32, count: u32, src: []const i16) ReplaceError!void {
    // invariant: the length never claims more samples than the buffer holds — the ground both sub-edits stand on.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);

    // invariant: the payload never exceeds the whole timeline bound — src comes from a caller slice, so bound it once against max_clip before it names a u32 size.
    if (src.len > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadRange;
    const ins_len: u32 = @intCast(src.len);

    // invariant: the removed span lies wholly within the current samples (checked without underflow — count against the remainder), refused by name before any write.
    if (at > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - at) return error.BadRange;

    // invariant: the result fits the fixed buffer — the length after removal is len − count, and the insert must fit the remaining headroom (checked without underflow, so the
    //            ClipFull refusal lands BEFORE the cut mutates a single sample rather than after).
    const headroom: u32 = timeline.max_clip - clip.len + count;
    if (ins_len > headroom) return error.ClipFull;

    // Every check passed, so each composed sub-edit is pre-validated to succeed — cut removes the span, splice fills it, and no refusal can land mid-edit.
    try timeline.cut(clip, at, count);
    try timeline.splice(clip, at, src);

    // invariant: the clip changed length by exactly src.len − count and never past its bound.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE REPLACE LAW — the span becomes src, the head untouched, the tail follows the new audio, the length is len − count + src.len (shorter, longer, equal); THE
//     GENERALIZATION LAW — empty src equals ALES2's cut, zero count equals ALES2's splice, src.len == count equals ALES114's paste_over, each byte for byte; THE LENGTH LAW — the
//     length changes by exactly src.len − count in all three directions; THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW — replace(at, 0, empty) the identity, a span past the samples BadRange, a
//     result past the buffer ClipFull ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE REPLACE LAW — the span becomes src, the head untouched, the tail after the removed span follows the new audio, the length is len − count + src.len. Proven three ways:
    //    a SHORTER replacement (the clip tightens), a LONGER one (it lengthens), and an EQUAL one (the length holds).
    {
        // shorter: remove 3 samples at 1, insert 1 — length 5 → 3.
        var shorter = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&shorter, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try replace(&shorter, 1, 3, &[_]i16{77});
        if (shorter.len != 3) return error.ShorterLength;
        if (!eq(shorter.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 77, 50 })) return error.ShorterWrong;

        // longer: remove 1 sample at 1, insert 3 — length 4 → 6.
        var longer = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&longer, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 });
        try replace(&longer, 1, 1, &[_]i16{ 71, 72, 73 });
        if (longer.len != 6) return error.LongerLength;
        if (!eq(longer.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 71, 72, 73, 30, 40 })) return error.LongerWrong;

        // equal: remove 2 at 1, insert 2 — length holds at 5.
        var equal = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&equal, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try replace(&equal, 1, 2, &[_]i16{ 88, 99 });
        if (equal.len != 5) return error.EqualLength;
        if (!eq(equal.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 88, 99, 40, 50 })) return error.EqualWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE GENERALIZATION LAW — replace generalizes three proven rungs; each corner is proven byte for byte against its own rung over one anchor.
    {
        const seed = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };

        // empty src → pure cut: replace(at, count, {}) equals ALES2's cut(at, count).
        var r_cut = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&r_cut, &seed);
        try replace(&r_cut, 1, 2, &[_]i16{});
        var c_cut = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&c_cut, &seed);
        try timeline.cut(&c_cut, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(r_cut.samples(), c_cut.samples())) return error.CutCornerBroken;

        // zero count → pure splice: replace(at, 0, src) equals ALES2's splice(at, src).
        const ins = [_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 };
        var r_spl = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&r_spl, &seed);
        try replace(&r_spl, 2, 0, &ins);
        var s_spl = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&s_spl, &seed);
        try timeline.splice(&s_spl, 2, &ins);
        if (!eq(r_spl.samples(), s_spl.samples())) return error.SpliceCornerBroken;

        // src.len == count → paste_over: replace(at, n, src) with src.len == n equals ALES114's paste_over(at, src).
        const paint = [_]i16{ 61, 62 };
        var r_pov = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&r_pov, &seed);
        try replace(&r_pov, 1, 2, &paint);
        var p_pov = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&p_pov, &seed);
        try paste_over.paste_over(&p_pov, 1, &paint);
        if (!eq(r_pov.samples(), p_pov.samples())) return error.PasteOverCornerBroken;
    }

    // 3. THE LENGTH LAW — the length changes by exactly src.len − count over one anchor: it shrinks, grows, and holds by the arithmetic alone.
    {
        const seed = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 }; // len 6

        var shrink = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&shrink, &seed);
        try replace(&shrink, 2, 3, &[_]i16{9}); // −3 +1 → 4
        if (shrink.len != 6 - 3 + 1) return error.ShrinkArithmetic;

        var grow = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&grow, &seed);
        try replace(&grow, 2, 1, &[_]i16{ 9, 9, 9, 9 }); // −1 +4 → 9
        if (grow.len != 6 - 1 + 4) return error.GrowArithmetic;

        var hold = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&hold, &seed);
        try replace(&hold, 2, 2, &[_]i16{ 9, 9 }); // −2 +2 → 6
        if (hold.len != 6) return error.HoldArithmetic;
    }

    // 4a. THE DEGENERATE LAW — replace(at, 0, empty) removes nothing and inserts nothing: the identity.
    {
        var idc = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&idc, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 });
        try replace(&idc, 1, 0, &[_]i16{});
        if (!eq(idc.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.IdentityMoved;
    }

    // 4b. THE REFUSAL LAW — a removed span past the samples refuses BadRange, and a result past the buffer refuses ClipFull, each before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });

        // at past the samples → BadRange, nothing written.
        if (replace(&clip, 4, 0, &[_]i16{9})) |_| return error.AtNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        // count past the remainder → BadRange, nothing written.
        if (replace(&clip, 2, 3, &[_]i16{9})) |_| return error.CountNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedCallWrote;
    }

    // 4c. THE REFUSAL LAW (headroom) — a result wider than the buffer refuses ClipFull BEFORE the cut mutates a sample: fill the clip to its bound, then a replace that removes one
    //     and inserts three would end one past the buffer.
    {
        var full = timeline.Clip{};
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < timeline.max_clip) : (i += 1) full.buf[i] = @intCast(@mod(i, 7));
        full.len = timeline.max_clip;
        const before = full.buf[0]; // the first sample the pre-cut would touch, kept to prove no write landed

        if (replace(&full, 0, 1, &[_]i16{ 100, 101, 102 })) |_| return error.FullNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.ClipFull) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (full.len != timeline.max_clip) return error.FullLengthMoved;
        if (full.buf[0] != before) return error.FullCallWrote;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-replace: REPLACE — remove the `count` samples starting at `at` and insert `src` in their place, IN PLACE, so [at, at+src.len) becomes `src`, the head " ++
        "untouched, the tail after the removed span following the new audio, the length becoming len − count + src.len. This is the GENERAL span-edit — exactly ALES2's cut(at, " ++
        "count) then splice(at, src) composed over its own public API, the paste-into-a-selection every editor ships: fix a flubbed bar with a shorter clean take and the song " ++
        "tightens, drop a fuller phrase over a thin one and it lengthens. All bounds are checked before any write — BadRange when the removed span falls outside the samples, " ++
        "ClipFull when the result would run past the buffer — then the two pre-validated sub-edits run so no refusal lands mid-edit. The crux is four laws. THE REPLACE LAW: the " ++
        "span becomes src, the head untouched, the tail follows, the length is len − count + src.len (shorter, longer, equal). THE GENERALIZATION LAW: empty src equals cut, zero " ++
        "count equals splice, src.len == count equals ALES114's paste_over, each byte for byte — one law, three faces. THE LENGTH LAW: the length changes by exactly src.len − " ++
        "count in all three directions. THE DEGENERATE / REFUSAL LAW: replace(at, 0, empty) the identity, a span past the samples BadRange, a result past the buffer ClipFull. " ++
        "Purely local — it removes an existing span and writes caller-supplied samples in its place, reading no byte past len; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, " ++
        "no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
